\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present our empirical findings in five parts: strategy-dependent compound severity (\S\ref{sec:strategy-severity}), phase-modulated amplification (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}), the phase-strategy crossover (\S\ref{sec:crossover}), cross-model replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), and statistical validation (\S\ref{sec:stat-validation}).


\subsection{Experimental Overview}
\label{sec:exp-overview}

We evaluate compound sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B using a 50-problem stratified subset of MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:dataset}). For each problem, we measure perplexity under four conditions---dense, attention-only pruning, KV-only eviction, and compound (both)---at sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. We compare importance-based and random eviction strategies (\S\ref{sec:sparsity-config}), and segment each generated sequence into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity reasoning phases via quantile-based attention sparsity partitioning (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). The interaction index (II, Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) quantifies compound super-additivity, and the severity gradient ($G$, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) captures phase-dependent amplification. All models are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} precision. Full experimental details appear in \S\ref{sec:experimental-setup}.


\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Severity (C1)}
\label{sec:strategy-severity}

The aggregate interaction index reveals a stark dependence on eviction strategy. Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii} reports the problem-level mean II across all 50 problems for each strategy and sparsity level. Under importance-based eviction, compound sparsity produces substantial super-additive degradation that grows with sparsity: mean II increases from \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s015} at $s{=}0.15$ to \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s030} at $s{=}0.30$. Under random eviction, compound interaction remains negligible across all sparsity levels, with mean II near \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_range}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Aggregate interaction index (II) by eviction strategy and sparsity level. Values are problem-level means across 50 MATH-500 problems on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B.}
\label{tab:aggregate-ii}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
Importance & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s020} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s025} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s030} \\
Random     & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_s020} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_s025} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_s030} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This strategy dependence indicates that compound sparsity severity is not an intrinsic property of the compression level. Rather, the same sparsity configuration produces qualitatively different compound behavior depending on the eviction strategy---an emergent phase-strategy interaction whose structure we unpack in the following subsections.


\subsection{Phase-Modulated Amplification (C2)}
\label{sec:phase-amplification}

Decomposing the interaction index by reasoning phase reveals that the strategy-dependent severity observed in \S\ref{sec:strategy-severity} is driven by amplification concentrated in the low-sparsity phase. Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports per-phase median II and the severity gradient $G$ at each sparsity level.

Under importance-based eviction, the severity gradient---the ratio of low-phase to high-phase median II---increases with sparsity from \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_s015} at $s{=}0.15$ to \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_s030} at $s{=}0.30$, spanning a \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_min}--\placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_max}$\times$ range. The low-sparsity phase---corresponding to exploratory reasoning with broadly distributed attention---bears disproportionate compound degradation, while the high-sparsity phase---corresponding to structured computation with focused attention---experiences comparatively mild interaction. Under random eviction, the gradient remains near unity ($G \approx$ \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: G_range}) at all sparsity levels, confirming that phase modulation is specific to the emergent interaction between head pruning and importance-based scoring.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Interaction index (II) by eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level. II values are problem-level medians across 50 problems. The severity gradient $G = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} / \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ quantifies phase-dependent amplification of compound interaction.}
\label{tab:main-results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Strategy & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}}$ & $G$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0.15 & Importance & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: median_II_high_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: median_II_low_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_s015} & [\placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_CI_lo_s015}, \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_CI_hi_s015}] \\
0.15 & Random     & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: median_II_high_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: median_II_low_s015} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: G_s015} & [\placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: G_CI_lo_s015}, \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: G_CI_hi_s015}] \\
\addlinespace
0.20 & Importance & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: median_II_high_s020} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: median_II_low_s020} & \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_s020} & [\placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_CI_lo_s020}, \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: G_CI_hi_s020}] \\
